{
  "Version": "2012-10-17",
  "Statement": [
    {
      "Sid": "DenyNonApprovedRegions",
      "Effect": "Deny",
      "Action": "*",
      "Resource": "*",
      "Condition": {
        "StringNotIn": {
          "aws:RequestedRegion": [
            "eu-west-1",
            "af-south-1"
          ]
        },
        "StringNotEquals": {
          "aws:PrincipalARN": "arn:aws:iam::*:role/GlobalBreakGlassRole"
        }
      }
    }
  ]
}

/*
  WHY THIS SCP EXISTS:
  Data residency control — ensures all AWS resources are created only in
  approved regions. Any API call requesting a resource in a non-approved
  region is blocked before AWS provisions anything.

  Approved regions:
  - af-south-1 (Cape Town) — primary region for African data residency
  - eu-west-1 (Ireland)    — EU data residency, GDPR-compliant

  IMPORTANT CAVEAT — GLOBAL SERVICES:
  The aws:RequestedRegion condition does not apply to globally-scoped services:
  IAM, CloudFront, Route 53, STS, AWS Organizations, AWS Support.
  These services operate globally and are unaffected by this SCP.
  This is expected and correct behaviour — IAM has no regional scope.

  BREAK-GLASS EXEMPTION:
  The GlobalBreakGlassRole is excluded from this restriction.
  Use only in disaster recovery scenarios where an approved region
  is unavailable and operations must temporarily continue in another.
  Access to this role should be tightly controlled and audited.

  To restrict to af-south-1 ONLY (e.g. South African data sovereignty):
  Remove "eu-west-1" from the approved region list.

  Attach at Root level for organisation-wide enforcement,
  or at specific OUs if only certain accounts require the restriction.
*/
